Q13: Why "ruby".equal?("ruby") => false and "ruby".equal?("ruby") => true

In Ruby, every string literal creates a new object in memory, while integers are performance-optimized immediate values.
Here is exactly why they behave differently under the .equal? method.
## 1. The .equal? Method Checks Identity
The .equal? method checks object identity. It returns true only if both sides point to the exact same object slot in memory. It does not check if the values inside the objects are equal.
## 2. Strings Are Mutable (Changeable)
Every time you type "ruby", Ruby allocates a brand-new space in memory for a new string object.

* The first "ruby" lives at one memory address.
* The second "ruby" lives at a completely different address.
* Because they are two distinct objects, .equal? returns false.

## 3. Integers Are Immutable and Singletons
For performance, Ruby handles numbers differently.

* Small integers are stored directly inside the variable pointer (immediate values).
* There is only ever one object in memory representing the number 1.
* Every time you use 1 anywhere in your code, it points to that exact same memory slot.
* Because both sides refer to the identical object, .equal? returns true.

