Yes, Ruby has a filter method (aliased as select), but it is used to keep elements that match a condition, not delete them.
To delete elements based on a condition, you should use reject or delete_if instead. [1, 2] 
## 1. The Opposites: filter vs reject (Returns a new array)
These methods do not change your original array; they return a brand new, modified array. [3, 4, 5, 6] 

* filter / select: Keeps elements where the block returns true.

numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
numbers.filter { |n| n.even? } # => [2, 4]

* reject: Deletes/filters out elements where the block returns true.

numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
numbers.reject { |n| n.even? } # => [1, 3, 5]


## 2. Modifying the Original Array (In-place deletion)
If you want to permanently mutate and delete elements from the original array, use the bang ! methods or delete_if.

* filter! / select!: Permanently keeps only matching elements.
* reject! / delete_if: Permanently deletes matching elements.

numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
numbers.delete_if { |n| n > 3 } # numbers is now permanently [1, 2, 3]

[7, 8, 9] 

## Quick Summary

* Use reject if you want a new array without the deleted items.
* Use delete_if if you want to alter the original array directly. [10, 11] 

